## 🔗 Stateless Web Tier

Move state (session data) OUT of web servers → store in persistent storage.

### Stateful Architecture (Bad)
```
User A → must always hit → Server 1 (has User A's session)
User B → must always hit → Server 2 (has User B's session)
```
Problem: Sticky sessions = hard to scale, single point of failure

### Stateless Architecture (Good)
```
User A ──→ Any Web Server ──→ Shared Data Store (Redis/NoSQL)
User B ──→ Any Web Server ──→ (fetch state from here)
User C ──→ Any Web Server ──→
```
Benefits: **Simpler, more robust, auto-scalable**

---

## 🌍 Data Centers

Geo-routing users to closest data center:

```
geoDNS
  ├── US-East Data Center (x% traffic)
  └── US-West Data Center ((100-x)% traffic)
```

**Failover:** If one DC goes down → 100% traffic routed to healthy DC

**Challenges:**
| Challenge | Solution |
|-----------|----------|
| **Traffic redirection** | GeoDNS routes to nearest DC |
| **Data synchronization** | Async multi-DC replication |
| **Test & deployment** | Automated deployment tools for consistency |

